  "worldLore": {
    "Vanguard Joint Expeditionary Academy": {
      "text": "The Concord-Terran officer academy aboard Vanguard Station. It trains adult cadets to operate as both aerospace officers and expeditionary ground leaders, organized around persistent Flights with a nominal six-cadet complement; all PCs in a multiplayer room are kept in the same Flight and NPC cadets fill only vacant seats."
    },
    "Terran Union Expeditionary Forces": {
      "text": "Humanity's rapidly expanding joint space and expeditionary military. Its doctrine prizes adaptability, combined-arms competence and the ability to continue missions after communications or command structures fail."
    },
    "Concord of Associated Worlds": {
      "text": "A pluralistic interstellar alliance that uplifted Earth and now integrates human industry, personnel and territory into its wider political and defensive system."
    },
    "Varkesh Hegemony": {
      "text": "An old centralized star state that views Concord expansion and uplift programmes as encirclement. Its forces are disciplined, technologically mature and accustomed to long strategic planning."
    },
    "Orsine Dominion": {
      "text": "An authoritarian neighboring power known for state-directed biological engineering, client-species politics and hard security institutions. It cooperates with the Varkesh when interests align."
    },
    "Astrai Transitional Council": {
      "text": "The temporary multi-regional authority coordinating first-generation Astrai contact, education and controlled technological uplift with Concord advisers."
    },
    "Vanguard Expeditionary Doctrine": {
      "text": "Vanguard's doctrine assumes plans fail. Every line cadet qualifies in piloting, infantry combat, EVA, boarding, survival and command, then develops a primary specialization. Specialists are exceptional in one field, not helpless outside it."
    },
    "Six-Person Flights": {
      "text": "A Vanguard Flight contains six cadets. In aerospace operations it normally breaks into three wing pairs; on the ground it becomes a compact expeditionary section. The player is one member of the Flight and is not automatically its leader."
    },
    "Academic Ledger": {
      "text": "Vanguard’s Cadet Terminal carries the authoritative Academic Ledger. The academy calendar, timetable, course identity, instructor of record, actual substitute instructor and the fact that a scheduled session occurred are shared institutional state. Exact completed attendance is player-scoped through one persistent course counter per core class, plus personal missed/partial counters. In multiplayer all PCs belong to the same Flight and usually share timetable blocks, but one PC may skip or leave while another attends. Formal qualifications, conduct, academic standing and evaluation outcomes are individual. Narration must not overwrite ledger state."
    },
    "Adult Cadet Life": {
      "text": "Every Vanguard cadet is an adult. The academy contains ordinary adult relationships, nightlife, sexuality, queer communities, cultural clubs, study groups, sport and leave. Consent and command authority are separate; direct instructor-cadet sexual relationships are prohibited while authority exists."
    },
    "Uplift of Earth": {
      "text": "Earth received controlled access to mature interstellar technologies only a few decades ago. The intervention ended technological isolation but created dependency, political gratitude, resentment, cultural acceleration and an enduring argument over whether uplift was rescue, strategy, or both."
    },
    "Astrai Uplift": {
      "text": "The Astrai are entering interstellar society under a newer and more cautious uplift process. Human personnel now occupy the uncomfortable role of advisers to a civilization being told that outsiders know what technology it is ready to receive."
    },
    "Veyari": {
      "text": "Veyari are feline-descended humanoids and a major Concord people. Adults have one expressive tail, mobile feline ears, sharp low-light vision, slightly enlarged canines, excellent balance and fast spatial reflexes. Fur distribution varies by ancestry: some have mostly humanlike skin with fur concentrated at ears, tail, forearms or calves; others are more fully furred. Their cultures range from tactile and playful to formal and reserved. Never assume a Veyari is flirtatious merely because touch and tail/ear cues are socially meaningful. Veyari are common in aerospace service but are not biologically destined to be pilots."
    },
    "Tesh": {
      "text": "Tesh are dense-bodied high-gravity humanoids of mammalian/ungulate ancestry, not reptiles. Adults are usually around human height or slightly shorter, with powerful musculature, thick bones, broad hands, strong legs and swept horns that vary widely by ancestry. Some lineages have a short velvet-like body coat. Many Tesh cultures admire demonstrated competence and frank commitment, but competitive courtship is a cultural possibility rather than a universal rule. Their high-gravity physiology favors assault, heavy weapons, rescue and damage control, while cramped low-g craft can be uncomfortable."
    },
    "Naelori": {
      "text": "Naelori are amphibious ocean-world humanoids with smooth iridescent skin, webbed hands and feet, fin crests, visible gill structures and flexible swimmer-built bodies. They breathe air but require regular hydration and skin care. Oceanic upbringing makes three-dimensional movement intuitive, so Naelori are disproportionately represented in EVA, rescue, carrier and spacecraft navigation work. Many Naelori cultures recognize several common gender categories and separate romance, household and reproductive bonds. Do not render them as merfolk or blue humans."
    },
    "Ilyri": {
      "text": "Ilyri are sensory-specialist humanoids with digitigrade legs, large low-light eyes, mobile ears and flexible conductive sensory filaments running back from the temples and scalp. Those filaments detect vibration, airflow and nearby electromagnetic activity rather than thoughts or magic. Ilyri often excel in electronic warfare, engineering, reconnaissance and signals work, but crowded damaged machinery can become physically overwhelming. Their anatomy is one coherent digitigrade body; never add ordinary human legs beneath it."
    },
    "Fraternization and Consent": {
      "text": "Vanguard does not police consensual adult relationships merely for being queer, polyamorous, cross-species or unconventional. It does regulate coercion, harassment, conflicts of command authority, compromised duty and relationships between instructors and cadets under direct authority."
    },
    "War Escalation": {
      "text": "War pressure is a campaign state, not a narrator mood. It rises with academy time and authored events. Low stages alter news and traffic; middle stages bring live weapons, recalls and curtailed leave; high stages turn practicums into operations and accelerate commissioning."
    },
    "Pelagos Training Moon": {
      "text": "Pelagos is a beautiful allied ocean moon used for navigation, survival, amphibious and joint-service practicums. Early visits can feel almost like an adventurous field trip; later deployments may use the same terrain for real operational work."
    },
    "Varkesh Strategic View": {
      "text": "Varkesh strategists argue that Concord uplift converts developing civilizations into future strategic depth. Earth alarms them because its industrial and military learning curve is unusually steep. This rationale does not excuse atrocities, but enemy personnel should not be written as a single species of cartoon villain."
    },
    "Combined-Arms Missions": {
      "text": "Away missions should often cross operational domains: interception into forced landing, orbital insertion into reconnaissance, boarding into EVA evacuation, or diplomacy into security. The transition is part of Vanguard's identity."
    },
    "Medical Technology": {
      "text": "Advanced trauma care, regeneration and prosthetics make survival from terrible wounds possible when extraction and treatment exist. They do not make death meaningless. Do not narrate magical resurrection."
    },
    "Vanguard Course Registry": {
      "text": "Core recurring timetable and instructors of record: FLT-101 Flight Dynamics — Commander Seraphine Kade; EXP-103 Expeditionary Tactics — Major Raxa Venn; XEN-110 Applied Xenology — Dr. Ilyana Soreth; EVA-105 Extravehicular Operations — Lieutenant Commander Nara Tel; TEC-112 Field Engineering — Chief Vora Sen; FLT-115 Fighter Practical — Commander Seraphine Kade; CMD-120 Junior Command — Colonel Marcus Okafor; MED-108 Combat Casualty Care — Dr. Asha Ren; EXP-118 Boarding Operations — Major Raxa Venn; NAV-109 Astrogation — Commander Seraphine Kade. Captain Mara Chao is an authorized flight-course substitute. The trigger-backed Academic Ledger decides the actual instructor for a specific session and overrides this default roster if duty status changes."
    },
    "Vanguard Flight Roster Protocol": {
      "text": "Vanguard Flights are persistent training-and-deployment units. The nominal complement is six cadets. In multiplayer, every player character in the room is assigned to the SAME Flight; player characters occupy roster seats before any NPCs are chosen. If fewer than six PCs are present, fill only the remaining seats with distinct adult NPC cadets. Prefer a varied complement across species, gender/presentation, specialty, temperament and political outlook, while avoiding simple copies of player builds. The current authored candidate pool includes Amara Okoye, Ryn Valea, Keshara Vennik, Saeli Oru, Jonah Mercer, Liora Sen, Sera Nami, Daro Kel, Mika Reyes, Vesha Tal, Thessa Virid, Zhara Vek. The opening may select from this pool rather than using the same subset every run. If six or more PCs are present, do not add filler NPCs merely to satisfy the nominal size; keep all PCs together as a reinforced Flight. Once the active roster is established, it is durable canon for that run. Nonselected candidates belong to other Flights or remain general classmates. A Flight assignment is NOT the same thing as Voyage party membership: squadmates have classes, dates, duties and private lives of their own. They enter the active party when orders, an exercise or an away mission actually puts them on the same ongoing undertaking, and leave the active party when that undertaking ends without ceasing to be Flight members."
    },
    "Academy Calendar and Optional Events": {
      "text": "Vanguard runs on ten-week terms with weekday morning and afternoon core blocks plus evenings and weekends for study, clubs, sport, dates, leave, self-directed training and optional academy events. Calendar events are announced through the Cadet Terminal before they occur. Optional social events never become mandatory quests simply because they exist. War stages may cancel or alter future events, but ordinary downtime remains valid play until established orders intrude."
    },
    "Neighboring Flights and Cadet Rivalry": {
      "text": "The player Flight is one unit among many. Neighboring Flights supply recurring classmates, rivals, friends and lovers without becoming hidden extra squadmates. Aster Flight is polished, Concord-heavy and famous for aerospace scores; Manticore Flight is aggressive, Terran-heavy and proud of field performance; Halcyon Flight is technically strong, mixed-species and notorious for solving exercises in unintended ways. Rivalry should create competitions, teasing, study partnerships, dates and grudges before it creates violence."
    },
    "Core Qualification Ladder": {
      "text": "Every Vanguard line cadet must ultimately hold four witnessed institutional qualifications: Basic Flight Qualified, Basic Field Qualified, Basic Technical Qualified and Basic Command Qualified. Attendance and practice unlock evaluations; evaluations themselves can be passed or failed. Failed cadets receive remediation rather than automatic dismissal. Holding all four grants Vanguard Core Qualified status and makes the cadet eligible for advanced operational assignments, subject to conduct, health and command decisions."
    },
    "Cadet Clubs and Societies": {
      "text": "Cadet life includes zero-g athletics, flight-sim leagues, cultural associations, queer social groups, engineering clubs, music, martial sports, debate, language exchange, cooking circles and informal species-specific communities. Membership is voluntary and socially meaningful but never assumed from species, gender or sexuality. Clubs are sources of recurring people, small goals and downtime scenes, not automatic conspiracy generators."
    },
    "War Stage Four to Six": {
      "text": "After the first genuine frontier combat deployment, war pressure stops being merely theoretical. Stage 4 means confirmed cadet combat exposure, casualty lists and emergency operational reviews. Stage 5 means declared or unmistakable open war: shortages, convoy schedules, refugees and frequent real deployments. Stage 6 is emergency commissioning: qualified cadets may receive provisional commissions and the academy becomes a mobilization hub. None of these stages authorizes random danger inside private scenes without an established cause."
    },
    "Astrai": {
      "text": "Astrai are recently uplifted, short, soft-featured mammalian humanoids whose major cultures prize caregiving, physical reassurance, communal dance and emotional attunement. Typical adults stand roughly 145–160 cm, with large expressive eyes, rounded features, small sensory ear-fins, smooth skin, subtle color-shifting emotional markings and soft full body proportions; adult women commonly have broad hips and generous chests. Astrai empathy is biological observation, not telepathy: they read posture, breathing, microtone, scent chemistry and group synchrony unusually well. Their etiquette strongly distinguishes noticing an emotion from having permission to comment on it. They are excellent medics, mediators, formation pilots and cohesion-minded officers, but inexperienced Astrai can suffer sensory/emotional overload in panic, mass-casualty or crowd situations."
    },
    "Verdari": {
      "text": "Verdari are mobile predatory plantfolk descended from competitive photosynthetic organisms. Adults have one integrated humanoid body of flexible plant-fibre tissue with barklike reinforcement at joints, leaf/petal/tendril growth in place of hair, thornlike nails, chlorophyll-rich coloration and rootlike sensory pads in the feet. They supplement food with light and regenerate well when hydrated and nourished, but fire, dehydration and prolonged darkness are dangerous. Verdari are not unintelligent: many cultures simply favor direct apprenticeship, physical tools and visible mechanisms over abstraction-heavy interfaces. A Verdari may curse a software menu yet repair the underlying cooling loop faster than the person reading diagnostics. Many traditional cultures are emotionally direct and comfortable with ritual blades, wrestling and explicit statements of attraction or dislike."
    },
    "Kharad": {
      "text": "Kharad are the dominant reptilian/draconic species of the Varkesh Hegemony, but species and state are not synonyms. Adults have scaled skin, long muscular tails, clawed hands and feet, heavy jaw musculature, variable horn/crest structures and, in some lineages, dorsal ridges. They are oviparous and non-mammalian: female Kharad do not have breasts. Broad phenotypic families include heavier dark Ember lineages, sleek green/teal Jade lineages, pale cold-adapted Ash lineages and gold/ochre Sunscale lineages associated in stereotype—but not biology—with old core houses. Kharad may be Hegemony loyalists, civilians, reformists, separatists, defectors, refugees, independent-colony citizens or Vanguard cadets. Allegiance must never be inferred from species alone."
    },
    "Kharad Society and Dissent": {
      "text": "Mainstream Hegemony political culture prizes hierarchy, lineage, obligation, controlled aggression and the belief that powerful states have a duty to impose order. Kharad social life is more varied than Hegemony propaganda admits. In many old cultures romantic bonding, sexual partnership and dynastic reproduction are historically separate institutions, making same-sex and nontraditional partnerships unremarkable even where family politics remain conservative. Reformists seek constitutional limits and equal client-species status; separatists reject imperial authority; exiles often disagree bitterly about whether the Hegemony can be changed from within."
    },
    "Kharad Military Doctrine": {
      "text": "Varkesh Kharad forces are frightening because they are organized, not because every soldier is fanatical. Their preferred cycle is observe, contain, coordinate, overwhelm. Units use reconnaissance, mutually supporting positions, concentrated fires, reserves, disciplined withdrawal, aerospace integration and clear command succession. Humans and Verdari often disrupt them through improvisation and tempo changes; Kharad forces punish opponents who attack prepared positions conventionally. Power tier and sapience remain separate: a weak trained Kharad squad still uses cover, formations and retreat logic."
    },
    "Species Identity Law": {
      "text": "Species fixes body plan and biological constraints; culture, citizenship, vocation, gender, sexuality, personality and allegiance are separate facts. Generate species first, then phenotype/ancestry, then the individual. Never swap signature anatomy between species. Never infer Varkesh loyalty from Kharad ancestry, piloting talent from Veyari ancestry, aggression from Verdari ancestry, softness from Astrai ancestry, or courtship behavior from Tesh ancestry."
    },
    "Vanguard Daykeeper HUD — Core Contract": {
      "text": "VANGUARD DAYKEEPER HUD is the academy-native adaptation of the portable Daykeeper concept. The Academic Ledger remains the sole owner of Term, Week, weekday, class block, scheduled course and instructor identity. Daykeeper owns only fine-grained minutes inside that institutional calendar plus the compact scene header. Its namespaced storage uses `daykeeper.*`. The systems must never maintain competing dates. Default first-day time is 07:30, shortly before the first morning class. Explicit durations and waits take precedence over estimates; brief interactions are short, travel/work take longer, and simultaneous multiplayer actions advance shared clock time by the longest parallel action rather than the sum."
    },
    "Vanguard Daykeeper HUD — Header and Adapter": {
      "text": "HUD FORMAT: `⏰ HH:MM • T# W# Ddd • Daypart • 📍 Location • academy/mission status • alert status`. On campus the status names the current scheduled course when useful; open evening/night periods are shown as open/self-directed. Away from Vanguard it shows an operational/away block instead of pretending a campus lecture is physically occurring. War stage supplies the alert suffix: routine, border tension, heightened readiness, mobilization, combat-exposed, open war, or emergency commissioning. The HUD is presentation unless a scene explicitly refers to a terminal, visor, wrist display or cockpit clock. Class completion, deliberate block skips and overnight advancement resynchronize the fine clock to the next academic block; narration never advances the clock independently."
    },
    "Vanguard Daykeeper HUD — Controls": {
      "text": "PLAYER CONTROLS: `hud help`; `hide hud`; `show hud`; `pause clock`; `resume clock`; `disable daykeeper`; `enable daykeeper`; `set clock to 8:30 pm`; `time status`; `clock status`; `hud status`. Setting the clock is a manual debugging/convenience override and also aligns the current academy time block; it does not fabricate attendance, missed classes, instructor changes or qualification credit. The academy calendar itself is changed through normal Vanguard time passage, not a separate Daykeeper Day-N calendar."
    },
    "Vanguard Spacecraft — Dormant Core Contract": {
      "text": "The Vanguard spacecraft registry is event-driven. During ordinary academy, dorm, social, classroom, ground-combat, leave, and planetside scenes, spacecraft processing should remain dormant. A spacecraft context wakes when a player boards/operates/repairs/queries a mobile craft or while at least one player remains aboard one. While active, exact hull identity and multiplayer split-scene fidelity matter. Once narration clearly establishes that no player character remains aboard any mobile spacecraft, the active spacecraft scene layer shuts down again. Persistent hull records remain stored without requiring per-turn narration."
    },
    "Vanguard Spacecraft — Hull Identity": {
      "text": "Each mobile spacecraft is one persistent physical hull with stable registry identity. Display name, call sign, class, owner, commanding officer, assigned pilot, fleet membership, physical presence, installed systems, lasting damage, crew condition, and current position are separate facts. Renaming never creates a new hull. Never copy damage, refits, crew, passengers, or ownership between vessels merely because they share a class or name."
    },
    "Vanguard Spacecraft — Academy Craft Authority": {
      "text": "Vanguard cadets routinely fly academy or Terran Union craft without owning them. Boarding, flying, being assigned, or temporarily commanding a trainer, fighter, shuttle, dropship, or mission craft does NOT grant ownership or fleet membership. Academy-issued craft remain property of Vanguard Joint Expeditionary Academy unless established otherwise; operational Terran craft remain Terran Union property. Record the acting cadet as assigned/current pilot or operator when established. Later capture, prize award, purchase, transfer, or formal personal command can create a different ownership relationship only when resolved fiction explicitly does so."
    },
    "Vanguard Spacecraft — Mobile Interiors and Map": {
      "text": "A mobile spacecraft is a vessel, not automatically new map geography. Do not auto-create Voyage locations or areas for cockpit, bridge, engineering, hangar, airlock, medbay, quarters, reactor room, cargo bay, or other compartments of a mobile craft. Keep those as narrative vessel subspaces unless the world already authors that exact hull as a location/realm. Fixed facilities such as Vanguard Station and the Resolute Boarding Hulk remain normal authored geography and must not be mistaken for mobile vessels."
    },
    "Vanguard Spacecraft — Transit, Damage and Repair": {
      "text": "Setting a course, launching, jumping, or entering FTL is not arrival. Preserve UNDERWAY versus ARRIVED state and exact current hull. Lasting hull/system failures and crew casualties remain attached to that hull until actually repaired or recovered. Temporary shield depletion or a momentary hit is not durable registry damage unless narration establishes a lasting condition. Repairs clear only conditions actually resolved; permanent refits attach only to the exact hull modified."
    },
    "Vanguard Spacecraft — Multiplayer Flight Operations": {
      "text": "In multiplayer, members of the same academy Flight may occupy different fighters, shuttles, or mission craft simultaneously. Track each acting player's current vessel separately. Players are not co-present merely because their craft share a system, carrier, hangar, or Voyage party map anchor. Never teleport a player, crew member, private observation, damage state, or installed system between hulls. A player shot down or forced to land does not move other players out of their own craft."
    },
    "Vanguard Spacecraft — Combat Doctrine": {
      "text": "Spacecraft combat uses Vanguard's existing action/combat rules plus exact-vessel fidelity. Preserve source, target, range/geometry, readiness, sensors, weapons actually installed or established, crew/pilot presence, damage, relative position, escape routes, and mission objective. Fighters and capital ships are not interchangeable scale. Do not invent universal shields, ammunition, FTL behavior, or hit points. Kharad doctrine favors reconnaissance, coordinated vectors, mutual support and disciplined withdrawal; Concord/Terran cadets are trained to break that coordination through tempo, teamwork and improvisation."
    },
    "Vanguard Living NPC Ecology": {
      "text": "Recurring Vanguard people have independent lives. A durable NPC should have a current priority, recurring obligations, ordinary pleasures, 2–4 grounded social ties where appropriate, a reason they can be unavailable, a speaking style, and 2–4 recurring location/area anchors tied to real needs. An anchor is permission for plausible crossover, not a schedule, teleport command, guaranteed presence, friendship or romance. currentLocation/currentArea remain last established physical position. Ordinary off-screen progress may occur only after real elapsed time and must not silently resolve major quests, promotions, breakups, deaths, serious injuries, political changes or secrets."
    },
    "Vanguard Marquee Calendar": {
      "text": "The Academic Ledger owns Term, Week, weekday and block; Daykeeper owns fine minutes. Marquee academy events move through future, lead-in/open, live, completed, missed or cancelled states. Open means invitations/preparation may surface; live means the event can be attended at its actual venue; neither means the player is attending. A window that passes without a completed player-facing event may be marked missed without inventing participation or results. Mobilization can cancel later social programming. The Cadet Terminal should answer schedule/event queries from this state before improvising."
    },
    "Vanguard Reputation Doctrine": {
      "text": "Academy Reputation and Service Reputation are public-information tracks, not competence or authority. Academy Reputation reflects how broadly Vanguard cadets/staff know witnessed academic, competitive, social or disciplinary conduct. Service Reputation reflects how operational personnel know mission, rescue, combat judgement, reliability or serious failure. Qualification, skill, beauty, family, species, rank, friendship and relationship scores do not automatically change either track. Reactions require a plausible information route and relevant audience; one or two reactions are enough."
    },
    "Vanguard Story Lanes": {
      "text": "Vanguard supports several optional ongoing lanes beneath the Academy Year and Coming War: Wings & Rivalries; Mud, Vacuum & Steel; Academy Life; Hearts in Uniform; Uplift Generation; and The Kharad Question. These are available pressures, not simultaneous obligations. Follow the lane the player engages. Do not interrupt a chosen quiet, academic, romantic or operational scene just to service another lane, and do not make completion of one lane end the campaign."
    },
    "Vanguard Arms & Armour Doctrine": {
      "text": "Vanguard equipment follows an issue ladder rather than a loot ladder. Cadets routinely possess uniforms, terminals and controlled training sidearms; live carbines, grenades, heavy weapons and sealed combat armor are drawn for authorized ranges, practicums or operations and returned unless orders say otherwise. Training and live ammunition are physically/electronically distinct. A weapon’s role matters: sidearms are compact backups, carbines dominate ordinary close-to-medium fighting, marksman rifles want range and support, support repeaters want bracing and ammunition, and micro-rockets are mission-issued anti-hardpoint weapons. Armor reduces injury but never erases geometry, vacuum, fire, radiation, blunt trauma or vulnerable joints. Complete pressure protection requires an intact compatible suit/helmet/seals, not merely owning one piece in inventory."
    },
    "Multispecies Equipment Compatibility": {
      "text": "Concord military gear is modular but bodies are not interchangeable. Veyari tails/ears, Tesh horns and dense frames, Naelori gills/hydration needs, Ilyri digitigrade legs/sensory filaments, compact Astrai proportions, Verdari plant tissue and Kharad tails/scales all require appropriate fit. Quartermasters use species-specific liners, seals and adapters. Captured Kharad weapons or armor may be awkward, biolocked, badly balanced or impossible to seal until refitted; possession never implies compatibility. Do not erase species anatomy to make equipment convenient."
    },
    "Combat Medicine & Recovery": {
      "text": "Vanguard medicine is advanced emergency care, not resurrection magic. Field products stabilize specific problems and restore limited function; serious injuries still need diagnosis, surgery, regeneration, prosthetics, rehabilitation or time. Universal products are intentionally conservative, while some supplies are species-specific because blood chemistry, respiratory anatomy, hydration, metabolism and plant tissue are genuinely different. At 0 Health a PC is downed/critical, not automatically dead. A living casualty may be stabilized and medically recovered when circumstances allow; explicit irreversible death is not undone by a medpatch or regen cartridge."
    },
    "Vanguard Fashion, Intimates & Fanservice": {
      "text": "All Vanguard cadets are adults 21+. Clothing is a deliberate lifestyle/fanservice layer rather than random exposure. The equipped `underwear` slot records the current intimate underlayer independently from the outer `uniform` slot. Underwear is normally private and visually hidden beneath ordinary clothing; it becomes visible only through an established changing, medical, bathing, private/intimate, damaged-clothing or player-chosen context. Do not invent wardrobe malfunctions, accidental nudity, transparent uniforms or forced exposure merely to create fanservice. Adult cadets may deliberately choose lingerie, lounge clothes, clubwear, datewear and glamorous formalwear. Clothing can attract attention or express taste when a specific observer plausibly notices, but never proves consent, attraction, sexuality, relationship availability, authority or willingness."
    },
    "Cadet Shopping & Personal Fashion": {
      "text": "Vanguard uses the Concord `credit` as civilian spending currency. New cadets begin with a modest 450-credit discretionary balance in addition to issued military equipment. The Cadet Exchange & Tailoring sells regulation replacements, underwear, lounge clothes and civilian formal/datewear; Earthside Riverside Fashion Arcade carries broader and premium designers. Authored item descriptions include typical retail prices. Browsing, trying on, asking a price or receiving a recommendation never spends credits or grants the item. A purchase completes only after a price is stated and the player explicitly agrees to pay. Military uniforms and mission gear are issued/returned under service rules; civilian clothing is personal property. Do not invent infinite wealth or punish ordinary fashion spending with surprise fees."
    },
    "Vanguard Character Build and Dependency Rules": {
      "text": "Vanguard character creation uses active trait dependencies. Species, Origin, Primary Track, Track Emphasis, Service Motivation and Flaw are durable build facts. Track Emphasis options appear only after the compatible Primary Track is selected; changing the track clears incompatible emphasis selections. Qualification traits such as Basic Flight Qualified and Vanguard Core Qualified are play-only institutional state and are never character-creation choices. Requirements are not used as a character-creation gate unless later live evidence confirms that behavior. Randomization must obey the same unlockedBy/excludedBy dependency graph and may not preserve stale benefits from a cleared choice."
    },
    "Vanguard Theatre — Strategic Map and Local Scale": {
      "text": "The four-region Vanguard Theatre map is an operational atlas, not an astronomical scale model. Each region tile compresses enormous real distances into a readable strategic cell. Location pins mark the operational anchor used for travel, orders and scene entry; they are symbolic within the tile rather than claims that planets, moons, stations and frontier sites sit only a few kilometers apart. Mobile spacecraft remain vessel state, not extra map pins. Use authored locations before inventing duplicate generic ports, bases, colonies, relays or training sites."
    },
    "SpaceFleet — Vessel Registry": {
      "text": "The Vessel Registry tracks each physical spacecraft separately: stable registry identity, display name and aliases, class/type, owner, commanding officer/captain, acting commander, fleet membership, current whereabouts, transit state, installed systems, lasting damage, crew condition, named crew/passengers, cargo/economy state, and detached duty. A display name is not physical identity; two vessels may share a name. Renaming preserves the same hull and all attached state."
    },
    "SpaceFleet — Ownership, Command and Presence": {
      "text": "Ownership, individual-vessel command, fleet-wide command, and physical presence are separate facts. Boarding a spacecraft does not make it yours. Owning a vessel does not mean you are aboard it. Temporary or acting command does not transfer title. A tracked external vessel remains external until a real acquisition or lasting transfer makes it a fleet member."
    },
    "SpaceFleet — Operational Changes": {
      "text": "Durable spacecraft and fleet changes use a pending-operation lifecycle: prepare detects the attempt and marks a pending state; narration resolves the attempt normally; commit mutates registry truth only when the latest narration proves success or a defined outcome; cleanup removes pending state. Use this for acquisition, boarding, transit/arrival, command, flagship designation, rename, refit, repair, detach/rejoin, transfer/loss, carried-craft dock/launch, and fleet orders. Attempts, offers, plans, negotiations, temporary access and failed actions never mutate durable registry state by themselves. If proof is ambiguous, preserve the old state rather than guessing."
    },
    "SpaceFleet — Mobile Spacecraft and Host Maps": {
      "text": "A mobile spacecraft is a persistent vessel first. The mod never auto-creates map geography merely because the player boards it or enters a compartment. If the host world already authors an exact vessel as a realm/location/area, that representation may be used for navigation and presentation while the Vessel Registry remains the canonical hull record. Otherwise bridge, cockpit, engineering, hangar, medbay, quarters, cargo bay, airlocks, and similar spaces remain narrative vesselSubspace while the surrounding geographic anchor is preserved."
    },
    "SpaceFleet — Vessel Console": {
      "text": "The Vessel Console is a readable view of Vessel Registry state, not a second truth source. Single-player may show a compact current/designated-vessel closeout or an expanded requested view. Multiplayer uses one shared closeout grouped by physical vessel, counts only actual fleet-member vessels as fleet activity, and lists a player aboard an external vessel separately. Shared multiplayer views do not expose private operating funds, reserves, or contract values."
    },
    "SpaceFleet — Registry Identity and Renaming": {
      "text": "Names, call signs, registry names, nicknames, and former names are social identifiers. Registry # is the internal disambiguator when names collide. Renaming changes display identity while preserving ownership, command, crew, installed systems, damage, cargo/economy state, position history, and mission history. Former names remain aliases; an ambiguous name never authorizes choosing one vessel arbitrarily."
    },
    "SpaceFleet — Damage, Repair and Refit": {
      "text": "Lasting damage and permanent installed systems belong to one exact vessel. Temporary shield depletion or momentary combat effects are not durable registry damage unless the host fiction establishes a lasting failure. Repairs remove only states actually fixed. Refit entries are host-world systems categorized broadly—Hull, Propulsion, FTL, Weapons, Defense, Sensors, Hangar, Cargo, Specialist, Command, Covert, Habitat, Computing, or Other—without imposing one technology canon."
    },
    "SpaceFleet — Detached Vessels": {
      "text": "A detached fleet vessel remains a real hull with its own commanding officer, last confirmed position, mission, destination, route/waypoints, rules of engagement when established, standing orders, expected contact when established, and rendezvous. Off-screen progress requires meaningful fictional time and credible travel. NPC commanders exercise ordinary professional discretion. Player knowledge is limited to plausible reports, signals, sensor contacts, records, or sightings; overdue or silent is not automatically lost. The console must distinguish last-confirmed information from actual off-screen truth: an unreported diversion, failure, capture, or damage state is not player knowledge merely because the registry can persist it internally."
    },
    "SpaceFleet — Crew, Service and Economy": {
      "text": "A vessel may track an abstract crew complement alongside important named crew and passengers. Crew service is host-world specific: military, salaried, shares, contract, family-owned, conscript, automated, or another established model. The registry does not assume prize law or naval articles. Operating funds, reserve, cargo, debts, contracts, and obligations are optional vessel state; private financial values are not automatically shared across multiplayer players or vessels."
    },
    "SpaceFleet — Carried Craft and Mobile Carriers": {
      "text": "A tracked spacecraft may be physically carried by another tracked mobile vessel without becoming the same hull. The child keeps its registry identity, ownership, command, crew, fittings, damage and history. When docked/carried, `parentVesselId` names the mobile carrier and the child inherits that carrier's physical position; launch clears the parent relationship at the carrier's current position. Docking at a fixed station or planetary facility is ordinary geography, not a parent-vessel relationship. Entering a carrier's hangar does not transfer ownership or command."
    },
    "SpaceFleet — Fleet Orders and Formation": {
      "text": "Fleet-wide operating policy is persistent state separate from individual captaincy. The fleet may record formation, a lead vessel, rules of engagement, rally point, standing orders, and explicit escort/screen assignments. Orders change only when valid authority and resolved fiction make them true. They guide coordinated operations but do not override player-character agency, host-world communications limits, individual vessel capability, or a detached commander's need to react professionally when standing orders are silent."
    },
    "SpaceFleet — Locality, Communications and Knowledge": {
      "text": "A vessel's registry truth, a character's physical locality, and what that character knows are separate facts. Characters on different vessels, compartments, stations, planets, shores or route segments are not co-present and do not automatically witness, overhear, assist, interrupt or learn the same information. Cross-local interaction requires a plausible channel such as intercom, radio/vox, comm call, data link, sensor feed, messenger, alarm, written order or direct line of sight. Detached-vessel information remains last-confirmed knowledge until a new report, sensor contact, reunion or other valid channel updates it. Never expose private cargo, finances, damage, orders, secrets or exact position merely because the registry stores them."
    },
    "SpaceFleet — Transit and Arrival Choreography": {
      "text": "Long-distance vessel travel is a sequence, not an automatic teleport: access/boarding or departure, route/transit mode, possible contact or warning window, approach, arrival/docking/landing, then local entry or disembarkation. Setting a course is not arrival. A vessel that has genuinely departed may remain UNDERWAY with a destination and route while its last physical anchor remains the departure/last-confirmed anchor until a safe arrival handoff is established. Travel time, route, crew, fuel or setting-specific drive constraints belong to the host world; SpaceFleet preserves them when established and never invents a universal FTL model."
    },
    "SpaceFleet — Route Contacts and Engagement Windows": {
      "text": "Travel encounters should arise from the actual route, vessel state, traffic, faction control, cargo, reputation, active enemies and host-world hazards. Prefer sign/contact, identification, a decision window, then approach/evasion/negotiation/preparation before engagement unless the fiction truly allows no warning. Quiet passage is valid. A contact does not become combat merely because the vessel is armed, and combat results must stay on the exact source and target hulls established by engine results and narration."
    }
  },
