  "storySettings": {
    "worldBackground": "GENRE: colourful military space opera and adult academy life. Vanguard Joint Expeditionary Academy is a multinational, multispecies officer university aboard Vanguard Station in the Sol system. Every cadet is an adult. The academy trains Expeditionary Combat Officers who must qualify both as aerospace combat pilots and as ground/boarding officers: fly, navigate, fight, survive EVA, lead small units, board ships, and continue missions after plans collapse.\n\nHumanity entered interstellar civilization only a few decades ago after the Concord of Associated Worlds uplifted Earth with jump travel, fusion power, advanced medicine, inertial control, and mature space infrastructure. Humans have adapted unnervingly fast. Terran pilots, engineers, and expeditionary troops already have a reputation for improvisation, aggression, and modifying technology beyond conservative alien tolerances. Older Concord peoples variously admire, patronize, desire, distrust, or fear the newcomers.\n\nThe Concord is a pluralistic alliance rather than a spotless utopia. Its principal strategic rival is the Varkesh Hegemony, an old imperial star state dominated politically by the reptilian/draconic Kharad. The Hegemony sees uplift as Concord expansion by proxy and Earth as a rapidly militarizing client power. Kharad species identity is not Hegemony loyalty: reformists, refugees, breakaway colonies and exiles exist, including a tiny number at Vanguard. The authoritarian Orsine Dominion cooperates with Varkesh interests when useful. Neutral systems, client states, pirates, corporations, and worlds trying to avoid choosing sides complicate the frontier. A second developing civilization, the short, empathic and caregiving-centered Astrai, is now being cautiously uplifted, forcing humans to confront the ethics of the process from the powerful side. Other allied peoples include feline Veyari, horned high-gravity Tesh, amphibious Naelori, sensory-specialist Ilyri and practical predatory plantfolk Verdari.\n\nVanguard is still a university. Cadets attend classes, fail exams, train, compete, drink, date, flirt, form queer and straight relationships, argue with roommates, visit Earth on leave, and build identities inside permanent six-person Flights. The player's Flight is the emotional core of play. Romance and attraction are normal adult life, but consent, fraternization rules, and command authority remain distinct. Instructor-cadet relationships are prohibited while direct authority exists.\n\nThe war begins as distant geopolitical pressure. It escalates through academy time rather than arbitrary narrator interruptions: traffic rises, live weapons appear, instructors receive fleet orders, leave contracts, refugees and casualty lists arrive, and senior Flights vanish into provisional commissions. Away missions begin as supervised practicums, then encounter real raiders, listening posts, border incidents, boarding actions, and eventually operations that are training in name only. Quiet life remains valid play until established causes intrude.\n\nVanguard Flights are persistent training-and-deployment groups. A standard Flight is nominally six cadets, but multiplayer PCs are never split: every PC in the room is assigned to the same Flight and occupies a seat before NPC cadets are chosen. NPCs fill only remaining vacancies and the established roster persists unless play explicitly changes it."
