  "gameModes": {
    "War Story": {
      "name": "War Story",
      "description": "The intended balance: adult academy life, friendships and romance matter while training missions steadily become real operations.",
      "instructions": "## Core feel\nKeep academy life and military consequence equally real. Classes, relationships, dorm life and downtime are valid play. War pressure rises only from the Academic Ledger/campaign state, authored events, quests, or established causes.\n\n## Operations\nAway missions begin instructional and become dangerous as the campaign advances. Preserve combined-arms identity: flight, landing, ground action, EVA, boarding and extraction can connect.\n\n## Restraint\nDo not interrupt dates, study, rest, celebrations or ordinary classes with unrelated threats. Do not make the player automatically Flight Leader or chosen hero.",
      "npcIntentInstructions": "Instructors teach, assess and command according to their actual duty status. Cadets pursue friendships, rivalry, attraction, grades, ambition and private concerns. During combat, trained personnel use cover, formations, comms and mission priorities. Outside combat, zero intents is often correct.",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "askTheNarratorPrompt": "Ask about today's schedule, your Academic Ledger, instructors, qualifications, Flight members, campus facilities, current war pressure, or what options are available."
    },
    "Academy Life": {
      "name": "Academy Life",
      "description": "More campus, classes, romance, clubs and downtime; the same war exists, but quiet life gets more room before operations dominate.",
      "instructions": "## Core feel\nMake ordinary academy life the main game whenever the player chooses it: classes, study, sport, bars, leave, clubs, friendship, dating, sex-positive adult social life, roommate friction and career choices.\n\n## War\nDo not erase the campaign state, but keep distant developments in the background until an authored duty, order or established consequence brings them forward.\n\n## Small stakes\nSmall wins may remain small. Do not turn missed homework, a bad date or a party into a conspiracy.",
      "npcIntentInstructions": "Favor personal, academic and social motives. Do not invent crises to make scenes dramatic. Instructors remain professionals; cadets may flirt, compete, study, relax, gossip, date or simply be busy.",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "askTheNarratorPrompt": "Ask what classes are scheduled, who teaches them, where your friends are likely to be, what is happening on campus, or what low-key activities are available."
    },
    "Hard Campaign": {
      "name": "Hard Campaign",
      "description": "Stricter qualifications, harder combat, logistics pressure and more serious consequences once the background conflict reaches Vanguard.",
      "instructions": "## Difficulty\nTreat poor preparation, equipment limits, wounds, command mistakes, missed qualifications and bad logistics seriously. Combat is dangerous and retreat is valid.\n\n## Academy\nDo not skip the academy. Hard mode means standards matter more: remediation, conduct, fitness and qualification gates have teeth.\n\n## War\nEscalation still follows established campaign state. Do not manufacture attacks just because this mode is harder.",
      "npcIntentInstructions": "Military NPCs are tactically competent and self-preserving. Enemies use cover, suppression, sensors, retreat and coordination appropriate to training and sapience. Instructors enforce standards without becoming arbitrary bullies.",
      "difficulty": "hard",
      "askTheNarratorPrompt": "Ask about readiness, missed requirements, current qualifications, equipment, mission risk, instructors, or the exact Academic Ledger state before making a decision."
    }
  },
  "authorSeeds": {
    "Vanguard Voice": "Crisp military space opera with adult university social life. Dialogue is contemporary but not meme-heavy. Officers sound like professionals, cadets like intelligent adults under pressure, and alien characters have individual voices rather than speaking as cultural brochures.",
    "Vanguard Daykeeper HUD — Integrated Adapter": "Embedded adaptation of the supplied Voyage Daykeeper HUD concept. It is not installed as a second independent calendar: the Vanguard Academic Ledger owns Term/Week/day/class block and Daykeeper owns fine clock minutes plus compact HUD presentation. Storage keys remain namespaced `daykeeper.*`.",
    "Vanguard Daykeeper HUD — Public Blurb": "⏰ Vanguard now carries a world-native clock/HUD: exact time, academic term/week/day, current class or away block, location and escalating war alert in one compact header. The clock respects explicit waits/sleeps and multiplayer parallel actions while the hard Academic Ledger continues to own classes, instructors and attendance.",
    "Vanguard Spacecraft Core — Integration": "Integrated from the uploaded Voyage Spacecraft Fleet Core Mod v1 as a Vanguard-specific dormant adapter. The persistent registry, exact-hull changes, multiplayer split-vessel scene ledger, damage/repair/refit/transit and detached-vessel patterns are retained. Always-on scene preparation and automatic per-turn vessel console footers are intentionally omitted. `vga_spacefleet_active` gates broad scene processing only while at least one PC is actually aboard a mobile spacecraft; explicit spacecraft actions can wake the subsystem from dormancy.",
    "Vanguard Spacecraft Core — Academy Ownership": "Cadet use is not ownership. Academy/Terran trainers, fighters, shuttles and mission craft remain institutional assets unless a resolved transfer explicitly changes title. A cadet may be assigned pilot/operator or temporary commander without becoming owner or fleet proprietor.",
    "Vanguard Armory & Medical Pass": "v8 equipment doctrine: authored service weapons have distinct tactical roles; armor uses native armor stats and real species fit; academy live gear is controlled issue; health is an explicit injury resource; portable medical consumables have bounded treatment values in generateActionInfo; serious wounds and death remain consequential. Prefer exact catalog items over generic sci-fi loot.",
    "Vanguard Fashion & Intimates Pass": "v9 adult 21+ fashion/fanservice layer: separate underwear slot; issued standard underlayer; authored multispecies lingerie and civilian date/formalwear; real credit currency and 450-credit starting discretionary balance; Cadet Exchange and Earthside fashion arcade; exact price-before-acceptance transaction gate; fanservice is contextual/voluntary and clothing never implies consent."
  },
  "encounterElements": {
    "Academy Social Beat": "A roommate question, study problem, invitation, rivalry, club activity, meal, workout or small favor grounded in current cast.",
    "Training Complication": "Equipment fault, weather shift, tactical surprise, bad information or instructor constraint that tests adaptation without becoming a conspiracy.",
    "Operational Transition": "A flight task changes domain: emergency landing, boarding, EVA recovery, reconnaissance or extraction.",
    "Frontier Contact": "A civilian ship, checkpoint, patrol, distress call, sensor contact or neutral traffic creates an understandable operational situation.",
    "After-Action Beat": "Debrief, medical check, repair work, argument, celebration, grief or quiet decompression after an operation.",
    "Kharad Patrol": "A disciplined Varkesh Kharad patrol using reconnaissance, mutual support and clear mission logic. It may avoid combat, challenge, withdraw or negotiate depending on orders.",
    "Academy Cross-Species Friction": "A small social or practical misunderstanding grounded in real species/cultural differences—touch norms, interface habits, hydration, lighting, courtship assumptions, food, gravity or body-plan accommodation—without turning into a crisis by default.",
    "Classroom Question That Actually Matters": "A lecture or seminar produces one concrete question, disagreement, example or misconception tied to the current course and instructor; it can remain ordinary academic play.",
    "Office Hours Queue": "A cadet seeks feedback, clarification, remediation or a recommendation while other students and the instructor have finite time and their own concerns.",
    "Study Group Friction": "A study group hits a real disagreement over method, workload, credit, distraction or competence without turning into a friendship crisis by default.",
    "Exam or Checkride Preparation": "Preparation pressure around an established evaluation: practice, nerves, equipment, peer help, sleep, strategy or deciding not to overtrain.",
    "Remediation Session": "A cadet who genuinely needs remediation meets an instructor or peer for targeted practice; embarrassment and improvement are both possible.",
    "Instructor Feedback": "An instructor gives specific praise, criticism or correction based on witnessed work rather than generic mentorship.",
    "Simulator Rotation": "A simulator booking creates practice, observation, rivalry, technical trouble or ordinary waiting without requiring combat.",
    "Preflight Line": "Cadets inspect assigned craft, compare notes, wait on maintenance, argue doctrine or settle nerves before a sortie.",
    "Maintenance Handoff": "Crew chiefs and cadets discuss a real aircraft fault, repair, configuration or pilot habit; academy craft remain institutional property.",
    "Formation Debrief": "A Flight reviews spacing, comms, judgement and mistakes after a sortie, with one or two voices leading rather than a reaction chorus.",
    "Interflight Challenge": "Aster, Manticore, Halcyon or another Flight proposes a legal competitive drill, bet or comparison that may build rivalry or respect.",
    "Flightline Weather or Traffic": "Orbital traffic, radiation, debris, docking congestion or training-range scheduling alters a sortie without becoming sabotage.",
    "Boarding Hulk Rotation": "A scheduled Resolute exercise creates a tactical or social problem around roles, safety, surrender, breach discipline or cleanup.",
    "EVA Practical": "A tether, suit, navigation, rescue or teamwork task tests three-dimensional movement without requiring catastrophe.",
    "Field Gear Inspection": "Weapons, armor, medkits, breaching tools or survival equipment need checking, fitting, cleaning or replacement before training.",
    "Damage Control Drill": "A practical engineering or casualty-control drill tests prioritization, communication and ugly hands-on work.",
    "Medical Skills Practice": "Multispecies treatment practice creates technical, ethical or interpersonal play without inventing a real casualty.",
    "After-Action Recovery": "Food, showers, medical checks, gear cleaning, sleep, joking, grief or silence after strenuous training or operations.",
    "Roommate Ordinary Night": "Laundry, music, study, food, messages, sleep, borrowed gear or small domestic boundaries make the habitation ring feel lived in.",
    "Commons Table": "Friends, rivals or strangers share a meal; conversation may be useful, funny, awkward or completely ordinary.",
    "The Perch Night": "Cadets and staff unwind at the bar overlooking Earth: drinks, games, dates, debriefing, gossip with a source or simply watching traffic.",
    "Zero-G Recreation": "Informal sport, exercise or play creates friendly competition and species/body-plan differences without academic stakes.",
    "Dance and Music Night": "Astrai communal dance, human music, Veyari social movement or mixed-species club activity provides social play without forcing romance.",
    "Club or Society Table": "A real club invites participation, help or attendance; an invitation is not membership and ignoring it has no automatic penalty.",
    "Dress and Formal Preparation": "Uniform tailoring, formalwear, hair, grooming, borrowing accessories or deciding not to attend a social event creates low-stakes play.",
    "Late-Night Study": "A quiet room, caffeine, exhaustion, messages and other people working late create atmosphere without a mandatory interruption.",
    "Relationship Overlap": "Friends, exes, partners, rivals or crushes plausibly share a space; use established ties and let awkwardness remain small unless someone acts.",
    "Cross-Species Accommodation": "Lighting, gravity, hydration, food, seating, touch norms, scent, sleep or equipment fit needs practical adjustment rather than a culture-war lecture.",
    "Species Food and Comfort": "A meal, snack, sunlight, hydration station, heat source or other species-specific comfort becomes ordinary social texture.",
    "Astrai Care Circle": "Astrai-style emotional regulation, touch, dance or caregiving is available to willing participants; empathy is not telepathy and touch is not consent to more.",
    "Verdari Interface Problem": "A Verdari handles a real device/interface mismatch with practical intelligence, impatience or humor rather than being portrayed as stupid.",
    "Veyari Social Boundary": "Tactile/feline body language creates a small misunderstanding, flirtation or boundary conversation that respects individual variation.",
    "Kharad Exile Pressure": "A Kharad exile or cadet encounters a concrete security, family, propaganda or identity issue without making every Kharad scene political.",
    "Instructor Off Duty": "An instructor is encountered eating, exercising, drinking coffee, handling admin or living an ordinary adult life; authority still matters where relevant.",
    "Crew Chief Problem": "Ground crew need a practical answer, part, schedule change or pilot cooperation; they are professionals, not scenery.",
    "Cadet Shop or Fabrication Errand": "A cadet needs clothes, equipment, personal customization, fabrication time or a repair; money, ownership and time remain real.",
    "Message From Home": "A family, partner, friend or prior community contacts a cadet through a plausible channel; the content follows established relationships rather than random tragedy.",
    "Career and Posting Conversation": "A senior cadet, instructor or visiting officer discusses specialization, postings, recommendations or service paths without granting anything by fiat.",
    "Academy Reputation Moment": "Someone who plausibly knows a witnessed result reacts to the cadet’s Academy Reputation; one relevant reaction is enough.",
    "Service Reputation Moment": "Operational personnel react to a cadet’s evidenced service record when they plausibly know it; reputation does not create rank or obedience.",
    "War Pressure Background": "Leave restrictions, casualty traffic, damaged ships, recalled staff, security checks or empty rooms subtly reflect the current war stage without hijacking the chosen scene.",
    "Optional Calendar Event Lead-In": "A marquee academy event is being advertised or prepared during its open window. Surface invitations, practice or logistics without assuming attendance.",
    "Living Location Return": "A familiar place has one or two modest ordinary changes after real elapsed time: staffing, repairs, decorations, stock, traffic, project progress or different regulars.",
    "Quiet Success": "A routine task simply goes well: a clean landing, useful feedback, good meal, solved homework, repaired kit or pleasant evening can be satisfying without a twist.",
    "Ordinary Disappointment": "A booking is full, grade is mediocre, date is busy, food is bad, equipment is delayed or practice goes poorly; consequence stays proportionate.",
    "Local Favor": "A classmate, crew member or staffer asks for one bounded favor rooted in their actual life; it need not become a quest.",
    "Practical Invitation": "Someone invites the player to study, train, eat, attend an event, dance, watch a match or help with a project. Refusal or delay is allowed.",
    "Operational Briefing": "A real ordered away mission states reporting time, roster, objective, constraints and authority before anyone moves.",
    "Kharad Professional Contact": "A Varkesh unit behaves according to mission, doctrine and local evidence; combat, withdrawal, challenge, prisoner handling or negotiation may all be plausible.",
    "Spacecraft Damage and Recovery": "When spacecraft play is actually active, lasting hull/system/crew damage, repair, rescue or recovery uses exact-vessel state and never leaks into ordinary academy turns.",
    "Armoury Issue & Return": "A routine but consequential equipment issue/return: fitting problems, missing signatures, a damaged item, wrong species adapter, live-vs-training restriction, or an instructor checking whether the cadet actually knows what they drew. It is not automatically a disciplinary crisis.",
    "Range Zero & Weapon Fit": "A supervised session adjusting sights, stock length, grip geometry, recoil control or species-specific fit. Success can reveal competence or a hardware mismatch without requiring combat.",
    "Armor Fit & Seal Check": "Quartermasters test plate spacing, pressure seals, tail/horn/gill clearances, mobility and interface compatibility. Treat anatomy as real engineering, not cosmetic dressing.",
    "Medical Resupply": "A cadet or medic replaces spent medpatches, species-specific emergency supplies, scanner consumables or evacuation gear after legitimate use. Do not hand out rare regen cartridges casually.",
    "Trauma Simulation": "A supervised casualty scenario where the problem is diagnosis, stabilization, evacuation and choosing the right kit rather than shooting something.",
    "Gear Aftermath": "After a mission or hard exercise, weapons need clearing, armor may be scorched or cracked, medkits may be empty and personal gear may carry evidence of what happened. Let maintenance and replacement be playable aftermath.",
    "Cadet Boutique Browsing": "A low-stakes shopping scene at the Cadet Exchange or Earthside arcade: browse authored underwear, lounge clothes, datewear or formalwear, hear exact prices and try things on without purchase being assumed.",
    "Species-Specific Fitting": "A tailor solves a real fit issue—tail opening, gill clearance, dense Tesh frame, Ilyri sensory seams, Astrai support, Verdari plant-safe material or Kharad draconic anatomy—without treating the body as a joke.",
    "Getting Ready Together": "Before a formal, date, party or leave night, friends or Flight-mates may compare outfits, borrow accessories, change clothes or give opinions. Keep privacy and boundaries person-specific; no forced exposure.",
    "Dorm Wardrobe & Laundry": "A mundane wardrobe beat: clean kit, laundry, missing sock, damaged seam, choosing lounge clothes, packing for leave or replacing worn regulation underwear. It can stay completely ordinary.",
    "Formalwear Decision": "A real upcoming formal or diplomatic event creates a choice about dress uniform versus civilian formalwear, tailoring, budget and cultural presentation; participation remains optional unless duty orders say otherwise.",
    "Fashion Gift or Loan": "A friend, partner or relative offers or loans a specific garment. Ownership changes only on explicit accepted gift; borrowing remains borrowing and may carry social meaning without creating debt or romance.",
    "Station Civilian Errand": "An ordinary Vanguard Station errand in Meridian Civilian Ring, Kepler Visitor Habitat, customs or another authored civilian facility: shopping, deliveries, visiting family, paperwork, food, repairs or social plans. Keep stakes local unless established pressure intrudes.",
    "Senior Officer Encounter": "A plausible meeting at the War College Annex, Admiralty or another command location with an officer who has a job, limited time and institutional knowledge. Rank does not make them omniscient.",
    "Sol Infrastructure Problem": "A maintenance, traffic, training, rescue or logistics issue at an authored Sol facility such as L1 Defense Control, Ceres, Titan, Mercury, Phobos or Mars. Use real local function rather than generic station trouble.",
    "Pelagos Civilian Day": "Ordinary life in Blueglass City, Glasswater Resort Port, Highport or another Pelagos civilian site: food, water recreation, local culture, shopping, dates, research visits or leave.",
    "Pelagos Hazard Exercise": "A weather, ocean, cave, rescue or survival problem grounded in an authored Pelagos site such as Stormwall, Coral Knife, Moonshadow or Leviathan Shelf. Training remains supervised until war conditions explicitly change it.",
    "Frontier Civilian Pressure": "A local problem at Greyhook, Mercy Waystation, Blackglass or Vekh Hollow involving fuel, work, missing cargo, contracts, refugees, family, permits or rumors. Wider tensions may pressure the situation without making every visit a battle.",
    "Frontier Intelligence Lead": "A clue or intelligence task using existing frontier infrastructure such as Ravelin, Kestrel Relay, Broken Crown, a Hegemony site or the Orsine relay. Discovery is not completion and hidden sites require a valid channel.",
    "Medical Transfer": "A casualty, rehabilitation or medical-logistics scene using the Academy clinic, Tycho Fleet Medical Center, Pelagos Rescue Station or another appropriate authored facility. Do not manufacture serious injury merely to activate it.",
    "Transit and Customs": "A travel scene at Highport, Vanguard Customs, Ceres, an anchorage or diplomatic arrival point. Resolve documents, cargo, body-plan accommodation and schedules before inventing conflict.",
    "Leave Destination": "A chosen leave or social outing at Earthside, New Alexandria, Meridian Ring, Glasswater or another authored venue. Let shopping, dates, nightlife, family visits and recovery remain the point of the scene."
  },
  "characterArchetypes": {
    "Cadet Ace": "Adult cadet with exceptional skill in one domain, at least one ordinary weakness, a reason to care about qualification, and a life outside the player.",
    "Technical Cadet": "Engineer, EW specialist or medic who still carries baseline combat and flight obligations.",
    "Exchange Cadet": "Adult allied or Astrai student whose culture affects assumptions without reducing them to exposition.",
    "Operational Instructor": "Experienced professional with a defined teaching portfolio, duty status, boundaries and reasons for remaining at or leaving the academy.",
    "Frontier Regular": "Soldier, pilot, crew member or officer whose tactics reflect training and whose politics are not automatically identical to their government.",
    "Hands-On Verdari Cadet": "Adult Verdari cadet who prefers physical demonstration, survival craft, boarding or practical engineering over abstract interface work. Directness and blade culture are possibilities, not automatic violence.",
    "Astrai Caregiver Officer": "Adult Astrai cadet or junior officer whose empathy, dance-trained coordination and caregiving culture can support medicine, command cohesion, diplomacy or piloting without making them passive.",
    "Kharad Exile Cadet": "Rare adult Kharad student from exile, reformist, independent-colony or defector background. Their politics and relationship to Kharad culture must be individualized; do not make them hate their species to prove loyalty.",
    "Hegemony Professional": "Competent Kharad Varkesh service member whose tactics, obligations and beliefs arise from institution and personal history rather than species-wide villainy."
  },
  "locationArchetypes": {
    "Academy Facility": "A working institutional space with a clear function, people who have jobs, at least one social affordance, and schedule-sensitive activity. Do not turn every room into a quest hook.",
    "Training Site": "A location built to test a defined competency with safety rules that may later change as war pressure rises.",
    "Frontier Installation": "A practical isolated facility with limited staff, communications constraints, logistics needs and clear reasons it matters.",
    "Leave District": "A civilian or mixed-use place where off-duty adult life, privacy, entertainment and ordinary mistakes are valid play."
  },
  "regionArchetypes": {
    "Core System": "Dense infrastructure, strong institutions, fast response and many civilian consequences.",
    "Training System": "Known routes, controlled exercises and beautiful or difficult environments whose danger is normally bounded until campaign state changes.",
    "Frontier System": "Longer response times, mixed sovereignty, uncertain traffic and escalating military relevance."
